p = "main.tex"
s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()

def rep(a, b):
    global s
    assert s.count(a) == 1, a[:80]
    s = s.replace(a, b)

i = s.index("The word compositional names two properties that these studies often merge.")
j = s.index("Answers to the representational question usually start from a principle about meaning.")
s = s[:i] + r"""The word compositional names two properties, and each comes from its own tradition. The behavioral tradition, from the systematicity of human thought \citep{fodor1988} to benchmarks of compositional generalization \citep{lake2018,hupkes2020}, calls a system compositional when its own outputs succeed on new combinations of familiar parts. The representational tradition, from classical constituent structure \citep{fodor1988} to tensor-product representations \citep{smolensky1990}, calls a system compositional when the state of a whole is built from codes of its parts, each part contributing the same code to every whole that contains it. The two properties can come apart (Table~\ref{tab:twobytwo}). A network can build every new combination from its parts and fail to produce it, and a network can produce correct outputs from states that no declared decomposition explains. Neither definition says how the parts come back out of a whole, yet both imply that they do. We therefore introduce \emph{recoverability}, the return of each part in its role from composites of parts fresh to the reading procedure. A procedure that the experimenter declares measures it in the states, the system's own procedure measures it in behavior, and the gap between the two on the same fresh parts dissociates the properties that benchmarks and probes each score alone.

""" + s[j:]

rep(r"\textbf{Contributions.} We define a representation as compositional when a declared readout recovers every part in its role from composites of parts fresh to the readout, and when each part enters every role through one shared code.",
    r"\textbf{Contributions.} We define behavioral compositionality by a system's own outputs on composites of fresh parts and representational compositionality by a state in which each part enters every role through one shared code, and we introduce recoverability by a declared readout as the property that separates them.")
rep(r"We show that language models whose states bind roles to word order answer questions about the roles by meaning, the reverse dissociation.",
    r"We show that language models whose states bind roles to word order answer questions about the roles by meaning, the reverse dissociation, and that image--text encoders show the first. We repair each failing procedure by training or reading it through the factorization the state already has.")

# --- Related work
i = s.index("\\textbf{Identifiability.} Additive decoders")
j = s.index("%=====================================================================\n\\section{Recoverable composition}")
s = s[:i] + r"""\textbf{Compositionality of meaning.} Frege's principle holds that the meaning of a complex follows from the meanings of its parts and the way they combine \citep{frege1892,frege1923}, and \citet{montague1970} made it a homomorphism from syntax to semantics. As a constraint on functions alone the principle is vacuous, because a recoding of the parts makes any assignment of meanings compositional \citep{zadrozny1994,westerstahl1998}. Conceptual combination strains it from the other side. A pet fish is neither a typical pet nor a typical fish \citep{osherson1981,fodor1996}, a conjunction can carry attributes that neither concept has \citep{hampton1987}, and \emph{red} contributes differently to \emph{red hair} and \emph{red apple} \citep{kamp1995}. Our role-factorization states the classical demand that a part contribute the same thing to every whole as a quantity, and its residual measures how far a system departs from it.

\textbf{Constituent structure.} \citet{fodor1988} argued that the systematicity and productivity of thought require representations whose constituents are tokened in every whole that contains them, and they challenged connectionist models to explain both without implementing a classical architecture. \citet{smolensky1987,smolensky1990} answered with tensor-product representations, whose constituents return by unbinding without appearing explicitly in the state, and \citet{vangelder1990} called such representations functionally compositional, requiring only general procedures that build and decompose them. Recursive auto-associative memories \citep{pollack1990} learned such procedures, holographic reduced representations fixed them \citep{plate1995}, and \citet{chalmers1990} transformed their states without decomposing them. \citet{fodor1990} replied that recoverable constituents need not act in processing, and the dispute over whether systematicity must follow from the architecture continues \citep{aizawa2003,quiltydunn2023,woydt2025}. Models of analogy bind fillers to roles explicitly \citep{hummel1997,doumas2022}. Our representational definition keeps the classical demand on the code, and recoverability tests the functional demand on parts that the reading procedure never saw.

\textbf{Compositional generalization.} Machine learning studies compositionality through behavior. Benchmarks score a model's outputs on new combinations of familiar primitives \citep{lake2018,keysers2020,kim2020cogs}, \citet{hupkes2020} divide the property into systematicity, productivity, substitutivity, localism and overgeneralization, and meta-learning brings networks to the human level on systematic generalization \citep{lake2023}. Models with similar accuracy on familiar data generalize very differently \citep{mccoy2020berts}, and success on new combinations can come from recombining memorized fragments \citep{dziri2023}. These tests leave the states unscored.

\textbf{Measuring structure in states.} Identifiability results guarantee generalization for additive decoders that an encoder inverts \citep{brady2023,lachapelle2023,wiedemer2024} and for linear, orthogonal factors \citep{uselis2026}, and our role-factorization is their additivity in affine coordinates. Probes decode constituents \citep{bernardi2020,redhardt2025}, yet decodable factors need not yield a decoder that generalizes \citep{montero2021,schott2022}. Tensor-product decompositions \citep{mccoy2019,soulos2020}, binding identifiers \citep{feng2024} and interchange interventions \citep{geiger2021,geiger2024das} can supply part codes, and compression measures score symbolic redescriptions of a representation \citep{elmoznino2024,elmoznino2026}. We add the requirement that a readout transfer to parts outside the vocabulary that fitted it.

""" + s[j:]
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)
print("ok")
